{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-66",
  "title": "Open Source Licenses",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Open Source Licenses"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This document provides a comprehensive overview of the open source licenses for the software packages utilized in Docket TV. Understanding these licenses is essential for ensuring compliance with legal standards and evaluating the implications on our ability to secure trademarks and potentially patent aspects of our intellectual property."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Below is a detailed list of each software package used within our platform, accompanied by its respective open source license, along with explanations of what these licenses entail and their legal implications for Docket TV."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Understanding Open Source Licenses"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Open source licenses are formal legal frameworks that dictate how software can be freely used, altered, and shared. These licenses are crucial for managing software rights and responsibilities, ensuring that developers can contribute to and evolve projects while maintaining legal clarity. Open source licenses come in various forms, each with its own set of permissions and constraints. Permissive licenses, such as the [MIT](https://opensource.org/license/mit) and [Apache](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0) licenses, offer minimal restrictions, promoting a broader dissemination and utilization of the software. On the other hand, copyleft licenses, like the GNU General Public License (GPL), require that any derivative works also be distributed under the same license terms, thus ensuring that the freedoms to use, modify, and share are preserved in all downstream derivatives."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Understanding Node Modules (NPM Packages)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Node modules are integral components of application development, consisting of packages of code that can be installed via the Node Package Manager (NPM). These modules, which Docket TV utilizes extensively, contain reusable code that enhances functionality and efficiency across various aspects of our platform. Each node module comes with an open source license that dictates its usage, distribution, and modification rights. By meticulously adhering to these licenses, Docket TV ensures compliance with legal standards and respects the intellectual property rights of the developers who have contributed to these modules, thereby maintaining the integrity and legal standing of our broadcasting platform."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Docket TV uses a variety of open source licenses for its software packages. Below is a detailed list of each license, along with a breakdown of what they permit and restrict for Docket TV."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. MIT License"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The MIT license is one of the most permissive and flexible open source licenses. It allows users to do anything they wish with the code, provided they include the original copyright notice and license. This means that proprietary adaptations of the software are possible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
